        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<P> Gypsy moth eggs were flown to determine how microgravity affects the developing moth's diapause cycle.  The diapause cycle is the period of time when the moth is in a dormant state and undergoing development.  Results from previous space-flights experiments with gypsy moths have indicated that microgravity may shorten the diapause cycle,  leading to the emergence of sterile gypsy moth larvae.  Since the gypsy moth is probably the most damaging insect pest of hardwood trees in the eastern United States, extensive ground-based research has been conducted to modify the gypsy moths' life cycle to create sterile moths.    <P>APPROACH:<P> Two different types of moth eggs were flown.  Half of the eggs were lab-reared and half were wild.  They were flown in the Shuttle middeck for 11 days.  After retrieval from the orbiter, the eggs were examined to determine the number of viable larvae and the status of the reproductive system.      <P>RESULTS:<P>  No results from the experiment are currently available.<P>
        </div>

